1003: 
1004:           $ sed -e '2c\' -e "$VAR"
1005: 
1006: ‘=’
1007:      Print out the current input line number (with a trailing newline).
1008: 
1009:           $ printf '%s\n' aaa bbb ccc | sed =
1010:           1
1011:           aaa
1012:           2
1013:           bbb
1014:           3
1015:           ccc
1016: 
1017:      As a GNU extension, this command accepts two addresses.
1018: 
1019: ‘l N’
1020:      Print the pattern space in an unambiguous form: non-printable
1021:      characters (and the ‘\’ character) are printed in C-style escaped
1022:      form; long lines are split, with a trailing ‘\’ character to
1023:      indicate the split; the end of each line is marked with a ‘$’.
1024: 
1025:      N specifies the desired line-wrap length; a length of 0 (zero)
1026:      means to never wrap long lines.  If omitted, the default as
1027:      specified on the command line is used.  The N parameter is a GNU
1028:      ‘sed’ extension.
1029: 
1030: ‘r FILENAME’
1031: 
1032:      Reads file FILENAME.  Example:
1033: 
1034:           $ seq 3 | sed '2r/etc/hostname'
1035:           1
1036:           2
1037:           fencepost.gnu.org
1038:           3
1039: 
1040:      Queue the contents of FILENAME to be read and inserted into the
1041:      output stream at the end of the current cycle, or when the next
1042:      input line is read.  Note that if FILENAME cannot be read, it is
1043:      treated as if it were an empty file, without any error indication.
1044: 
1045:      As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, the special value ‘/dev/stdin’ is
1046:      supported for the file name, which reads the contents of the
1047:      standard input.
1048: 
1049:      As a GNU extension, this command accepts two addresses.  The file
1050:      will then be reread and inserted on each of the addressed lines.
1051: 
1052:      As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, the ‘r’ command accepts a zero address,
1053:      inserting a file _before_ the first line of the input *note Adding
1054:      a header to multiple files::.
1055: 
1056: ‘w FILENAME’
1057:      Write the pattern space to FILENAME.  As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, two
1058:      special values of FILENAME are supported: ‘/dev/stderr’, which
1059:      writes the result to the standard error, and ‘/dev/stdout’, which
1060:      writes to the standard output.(1)
1061: 
1062:      The file will be created (or truncated) before the first input line
1063:      is read; all ‘w’ commands (including instances of the ‘w’ flag on
1064:      successful ‘s’ commands) which refer to the same FILENAME are
1065:      output without closing and reopening the file.
1066: 
1067: ‘D’
1068:      If pattern space contains no newline, start a normal new cycle as
1069:      if the ‘d’ command was issued.  Otherwise, delete text in the
1070:      pattern space up to the first newline, and restart cycle with the
1071:      resultant pattern space, without reading a new line of input.
1072: 
1073: ‘N’
1074:      Add a newline to the pattern space, then append the next line of
1075:      input to the pattern space.  If there is no more input then ‘sed’
1076:      exits without processing any more commands.
1077: 
1078:      When ‘-z’ is used, a zero byte (the ascii ‘NUL’ character) is added
1079:      between the lines (instead of a new line).
1080: 
1081:      By default ‘sed’ does not terminate if there is no ’next’ input
1082:      line.  This is a GNU extension which can be disabled with
1083:      ‘--posix’.  *Note N command on the last line: N_command_last_line.
1084: 
1085: ‘P’
1086:      Print out the portion of the pattern space up to the first newline.
1087: 
1088: ‘h’
1089:      Replace the contents of the hold space with the contents of the
1090:      pattern space.
1091: 
1092: ‘H’
1093:      Append a newline to the contents of the hold space, and then append
1094:      the contents of the pattern space to that of the hold space.
1095: 
1096: ‘g’
1097:      Replace the contents of the pattern space with the contents of the
1098:      hold space.
1099: 
1100: ‘G’
1101:      Append a newline to the contents of the pattern space, and then
1102:      append the contents of the hold space to that of the pattern space.
1103: 
1104: ‘x’
1105:      Exchange the contents of the hold and pattern spaces.
1106: 
1107:    ---------- Footnotes ----------
1108: 
1109:    (1) This is equivalent to ‘p’ unless the ‘-i’ option is being used.
1110: 
1111: 3.6 Commands for ‘sed’ gurus
1112: ============================
1113: 
1114: In most cases, use of these commands indicates that you are probably
1115: better off programming in something like ‘awk’ or Perl.  But
1116: occasionally one is committed to sticking with ‘sed’, and these commands
1117: can enable one to write quite convoluted scripts.
1118: 
1119: ‘: LABEL’
1120:      [No addresses allowed.]
1121: 
1122:      Specify the location of LABEL for branch commands.  In all other
1123:      respects, a no-op.
1124: 
1125: ‘b LABEL’
1126:      Unconditionally branch to LABEL.  The LABEL may be omitted, in
1127:      which case the next cycle is started.
1128: 
1129: ‘t LABEL’
1130:      Branch to LABEL only if there has been a successful ‘s’ubstitution
1131:      since the last input line was read or conditional branch was taken.
1132:      The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next cycle is started.
1133: 
1134: 3.7 Commands Specific to GNU ‘sed’
1135: ==================================
===== 1441-1670 =====
1441:    The following command replaces ‘hello’ with ‘world’ only on lines 1
1442: to 3 and from line 18 to the last line of the input file (i.e.
1443: excluding lines 4 to 17):
1444: 
1445:      sed '4,17!s/hello/world/' input.txt > output.txt
1446: 
1447: 4.2 Selecting lines by numbers
1448: ==============================
1449: 
1450: Addresses in a ‘sed’ script can be in any of the following forms:
1451: ‘NUMBER’
1452:      Specifying a line number will match only that line in the input.
1453:      (Note that ‘sed’ counts lines continuously across all input files
1454:      unless ‘-i’ or ‘-s’ options are specified.)
1455: 
1456: ‘$’
1457:      This address matches the last line of the last file of input, or
1458:      the last line of each file when the ‘-i’ or ‘-s’ options are
1459:      specified.
1460: 
1461: ‘FIRST~STEP’
1462:      This GNU extension matches every STEPth line starting with line
1463:      FIRST.  In particular, lines will be selected when there exists a
1464:      non-negative N such that the current line-number equals FIRST + (N
1465:      * STEP).  Thus, one would use ‘1~2’ to select the odd-numbered
1466:      lines and ‘0~2’ for even-numbered lines; to pick every third line
1467:      starting with the second, ‘2~3’ would be used; to pick every fifth
1468:      line starting with the tenth, use ‘10~5’; and ‘50~0’ is just an
1469:      obscure way of saying ‘50’.
1470: 
1471:      The following commands demonstrate the step address usage:
1472: 
1473:           $ seq 10 | sed -n '0~4p'
1474:           4
1475:           8
1476: 
1477:           $ seq 10 | sed -n '1~3p'
1478:           1
1479:           4
1480:           7
1481:           10
1482: 
1483: 4.3 selecting lines by text matching
1484: ====================================
1485: 
1486: GNU ‘sed’ supports the following regular expression addresses.  The
1487: default regular expression is *note Basic Regular Expression (BRE): BRE
1488: syntax.  If ‘-E’ or ‘-r’ options are used, The regular expression should
1489: be in *note Extended Regular Expression (ERE): ERE syntax. syntax.
1490: *Note BRE vs ERE::.
1491: 
1492: ‘/REGEXP/’
1493:      This will select any line which matches the regular expression
1494:      REGEXP.  If REGEXP itself includes any ‘/’ characters, each must be
1495:      escaped by a backslash (‘\’).
1496: 
1497:      The following command prints lines in ‘/etc/passwd’ which end with
1498:      ‘bash’(1):
1499: 
1500:           sed -n '/bash$/p' /etc/passwd
1501: 
1502:      The empty regular expression ‘//’ repeats the last regular
1503:      expression match (the same holds if the empty regular expression is
1504:      passed to the ‘s’ command).  Note that modifiers to regular
1505:      expressions are evaluated when the regular expression is compiled,
1506:      thus it is invalid to specify them together with the empty regular
1507:      expression.
1508: 
1509: ‘\%REGEXP%’
1510:      (The ‘%’ may be replaced by any other single character.)
1511: 
1512:      This also matches the regular expression REGEXP, but allows one to
1513:      use a different delimiter than ‘/’.  This is particularly useful if
1514:      the REGEXP itself contains a lot of slashes, since it avoids the
1515:      tedious escaping of every ‘/’.  If REGEXP itself includes any
1516:      delimiter characters, each must be escaped by a backslash (‘\’).
1517: 
1518:      The following commands are equivalent.  They print lines which
1519:      start with ‘/home/alice/documents/’:
1520: 
1521:           sed -n '/^\/home\/alice\/documents\//p'
1522:           sed -n '\%^/home/alice/documents/%p'
1523:           sed -n '\;^/home/alice/documents/;p'
1524: 
1525: ‘/REGEXP/I’
1526: ‘\%REGEXP%I’
1527:      The ‘I’ modifier to regular-expression matching is a GNU extension
1528:      which causes the REGEXP to be matched in a case-insensitive manner.
1529: 
1530:      In many other programming languages, a lower case ‘i’ is used for
1531:      case-insensitive regular expression matching.  However, in ‘sed’
1532:      the ‘i’ is used for the insert command (*note insert command::).
1533: 
1534:      Observe the difference between the following examples.
1535: 
1536:      In this example, ‘/b/I’ is the address: regular expression with ‘I’
1537:      modifier.  ‘d’ is the delete command:
1538: 
1539:           $ printf "%s\n" a b c | sed '/b/Id'
1540:           a
1541:           c
1542: 
1543:      Here, ‘/b/’ is the address: a regular expression.  ‘i’ is the
1544:      insert command.  ‘d’ is the value to insert.  A line with ‘d’ is
1545:      then inserted above the matched line:
1546: 
1547:           $ printf "%s\n" a b c | sed '/b/id'
1548:           a
1549:           d
1550:           b
1551:           c
1552: 
1553: ‘/REGEXP/M’
1554: ‘\%REGEXP%M’
1555:      The ‘M’ modifier to regular-expression matching is a GNU ‘sed’
1556:      extension which directs GNU ‘sed’ to match the regular expression
1557:      in ‘multi-line’ mode.  The modifier causes ‘^’ and ‘$’ to match
1558:      respectively (in addition to the normal behavior) the empty string
1559:      after a newline, and the empty string before a newline.  There are
1560:      special character sequences (‘\`’ and ‘\'’) which always match the
1561:      beginning or the end of the buffer.  In addition, the period
1562:      character does not match a new-line character in multi-line mode.
1563: 
1564:    Regex addresses operate on the content of the current pattern space.
1565: If the pattern space is changed (for example with ‘s///’ command) the
1566: regular expression matching will operate on the changed text.
1567: 
1568:    In the following example, automatic printing is disabled with ‘-n’.
1569: The ‘s/2/X/’ command changes lines containing ‘2’ to ‘X’.  The command
1570: ‘/[0-9]/p’ matches lines with digits and prints them.  Because the
1571: second line is changed before the ‘/[0-9]/’ regex, it will not match and
1572: will not be printed:
1573: 
1574:      $ seq 3 | sed -n 's/2/X/ ; /[0-9]/p'
1575:      1
1576:      3
1577: 
1578:    ---------- Footnotes ----------
1579: 
1580:    (1) There are of course many other ways to do the same, e.g.
1581:      grep 'bash$' /etc/passwd
1582:      awk -F: '$7 == "/bin/bash"' /etc/passwd
1583: 
1584: 4.4 Range Addresses
1585: ===================
1586: 
1587: An address range can be specified by specifying two addresses separated
1588: by a comma (‘,’).  An address range matches lines starting from where
1589: the first address matches, and continues until the second address
1590: matches (inclusively):
1591: 
1592:      $ seq 10 | sed -n '4,6p'
1593:      4
1594:      5
1595:      6
1596: 
1597:    If the second address is a REGEXP, then checking for the ending match
1598: will start with the line _following_ the line which matched the first
1599: address: a range will always span at least two lines (except of course
1600: if the input stream ends).
1601: 
1602:      $ seq 10 | sed -n '4,/[0-9]/p'
1603:      4
1604:      5
1605: 
1606:    If the second address is a NUMBER less than (or equal to) the line
1607: matching the first address, then only the one line is matched:
1608: 
1609:      $ seq 10 | sed -n '4,1p'
1610:      4
1611: 
1612:    GNU ‘sed’ also supports some special two-address forms; all these are
1613: GNU extensions:
1614: ‘0,/REGEXP/’
1615:      A line number of ‘0’ can be used in an address specification like
1616:      ‘0,/REGEXP/’ so that ‘sed’ will try to match REGEXP in the first
1617:      input line too.  In other words, ‘0,/REGEXP/’ is similar to
1618:      ‘1,/REGEXP/’, except that if ADDR2 matches the very first line of
1619:      input the ‘0,/REGEXP/’ form will consider it to end the range,
1620:      whereas the ‘1,/REGEXP/’ form will match the beginning of its range
1621:      and hence make the range span up to the _second_ occurrence of the
1622:      regular expression.
1623: 
1624:      The following examples demonstrate the difference between starting
1625:      with address 1 and 0:
1626: 
1627:           $ seq 10 | sed -n '1,/[0-9]/p'
1628:           1
1629:           2
1630: 
1631:           $ seq 10 | sed -n '0,/[0-9]/p'
1632:           1
1633: 
1634: ‘ADDR1,+N’
1635:      Matches ADDR1 and the N lines following ADDR1.
1636: 
1637:           $ seq 10 | sed -n '6,+2p'
1638:           6
1639:           7
1640:           8
1641: 
1642:      ADDR1 can be a line number or a regular expression.
1643: 
1644: ‘ADDR1,~N’
1645:      Matches ADDR1 and the lines following ADDR1 until the next line
1646:      whose input line number is a multiple of N.  The following command
1647:      prints starting at line 6, until the next line which is a multiple
1648:      of 4 (i.e.  line 8):
1649: 
1650:           $ seq 10 | sed -n '6,~4p'
1651:           6
1652:           7
1653:           8
1654: 
1655:      ADDR1 can be a line number or a regular expression.
1656: 
1657: 4.5 Zero Address
1658: ================
1659: 
1660: As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, ‘0’ address can be used in two cases:
1661:   1. In a regex range addresses as ‘0,/REGEXP/’ (*note Zero Address
1662:      Regex Range::).
1663:   2. With the ‘r’ command, inserting a file before the first line (*note
1664:      Adding a header to multiple files::).
1665: 
1666:    Note that these are the only places where the ‘0’ address makes
1667: sense; Commands which are given the ‘0’ address in any other way will
1668: give an error.
1669: 
1670: 5 Regular Expressions: selecting text
===== 2101-2335 =====
2101: 
2102: 5.6 regular expression extensions
2103: =================================
2104: 
2105: The following sequences have special meaning inside regular expressions
2106: (used in *note addresses: Regexp Addresses. and the ‘s’ command).
2107: 
2108:    These can be used in both *note basic: BRE syntax. and *note
2109: extended: ERE syntax. regular expressions (that is, with or without the
2110: ‘-E’/‘-r’ options).
2111: 
2112: ‘\w’
2113:      Matches any “word” character.  A “word” character is any letter or
2114:      digit or the underscore character.
2115: 
2116:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\w/X/g'
2117:           XXX %-= XXX.
2118: 
2119: ‘\W’
2120:      Matches any “non-word” character.
2121: 
2122:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\W/X/g'
2123:           abcXXXXXdefX
2124: 
2125: ‘\b’
2126:      Matches a word boundary; that is it matches if the character to the
2127:      left is a “word” character and the character to the right is a
2128:      “non-word” character, or vice-versa.
2129: 
2130:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\b/X/g'
2131:           XabcX %-= XdefX.
2132: 
2133: ‘\B’
2134:      Matches everywhere but on a word boundary; that is it matches if
2135:      the character to the left and the character to the right are either
2136:      both “word” characters or both “non-word” characters.
2137: 
2138:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\B/X/g'
2139:           aXbXc X%X-X=X dXeXf.X
2140: 
2141: ‘\s’
2142:      Matches whitespace characters (spaces and tabs).  Newlines embedded
2143:      in the pattern/hold spaces will also match:
2144: 
2145:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\s/X/g'
2146:           abcX%-=Xdef.
2147: 
2148: ‘\S’
2149:      Matches non-whitespace characters.
2150: 
2151:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\S/X/g'
2152:           XXX XXX XXXX
2153: 
2154: ‘\<’
2155:      Matches the beginning of a word.
2156: 
2157:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\</X/g'
2158:           Xabc %-= Xdef.
2159: 
2160: ‘\>’
2161:      Matches the end of a word.
2162: 
2163:           $ echo "abc %-= def." | sed 's/\>/X/g'
2164:           abcX %-= defX.
2165: 
2166: ‘\`’
2167:      Matches only at the start of pattern space.  This is different from
2168:      ‘^’ in multi-line mode.
2169: 
2170:      Compare the following two examples:
2171: 
2172:           $ printf "a\nb\nc\n" | sed 'N;N;s/^/X/gm'
2173:           Xa
2174:           Xb
2175:           Xc
2176: 
2177:           $ printf "a\nb\nc\n" | sed 'N;N;s/\`/X/gm'
2178:           Xa
2179:           b
2180:           c
2181: 
2182: ‘\'’
2183:      Matches only at the end of pattern space.  This is different from
2184:      ‘$’ in multi-line mode.
2185: 
2186: 5.7 Back-references and Subexpressions
2187: ======================================
2188: 
2189: “back-references” are regular expression commands which refer to a
2190: previous part of the matched regular expression.  Back-references are
2191: specified with backslash and a single digit (e.g.  ‘\1’).  The part of
2192: the regular expression they refer to is called a “subexpression”, and is
2193: designated with parentheses.
2194: 
2195:    Back-references and subexpressions are used in two cases: in the
2196: regular expression search pattern, and in the REPLACEMENT part of the
2197: ‘s’ command (*note Regular Expression Addresses: Regexp Addresses. and
2198: *note The "s" Command::).
2199: 
2200:    In a regular expression pattern, back-references are used to match
2201: the same content as a previously matched subexpression.  In the
2202: following example, the subexpression is ‘.’ - any single character
2203: (being surrounded by parentheses makes it a subexpression).  The
2204: back-reference ‘\1’ asks to match the same content (same character) as
2205: the sub-expression.
2206: 
2207:    The command below matches words starting with any character, followed
2208: by the letter ‘o’, followed by the same character as the first.
2209: 
2210:      $ sed -E -n '/^(.)o\1$/p' /usr/share/dict/words
2211:      bob
2212:      mom
2213:      non
2214:      pop
2215:      sos
2216:      tot
2217:      wow
2218: 
2219:    Multiple subexpressions are automatically numbered from
2220: left-to-right.  This command searches for 6-letter palindromes (the
2221: first three letters are 3 subexpressions, followed by 3 back-references
2222: in reverse order):
2223: 
2224:      $ sed -E -n '/^(.)(.)(.)\3\2\1$/p' /usr/share/dict/words
2225:      redder
2226: 
2227:    In the ‘s’ command, back-references can be used in the REPLACEMENT
2228: part to refer back to subexpressions in the REGEXP part.
2229: 
2230:    The following example uses two subexpressions in the regular
2231: expression to match two space-separated words.  The back-references in
2232: the REPLACEMENT part prints the words in a different order:
2233: 
2234:      $ echo "James Bond" | sed -E 's/(.*) (.*)/The name is \2, \1 \2./'
2235:      The name is Bond, James Bond.
2236: 
2237:    When used with alternation, if the group does not participate in the
2238: match then the back-reference makes the whole match fail.  For example,
2239: ‘a(.)|b\1’ will not match ‘ba’.  When multiple regular expressions are
2240: given with ‘-e’ or from a file (‘-f FILE’), back-references are local to
2241: each expression.
2242: 
2243: 5.8 Escape Sequences - specifying special characters
2244: ====================================================
2245: 
2246: Until this chapter, we have only encountered escapes of the form ‘\^’,
2247: which tell ‘sed’ not to interpret the circumflex as a special character,
2248: but rather to take it literally.  For example, ‘\*’ matches a single
2249: asterisk rather than zero or more backslashes.
2250: 
2251:    This chapter introduces another kind of escape(1)—that is, escapes
2252: that are applied to a character or sequence of characters that
2253: ordinarily are taken literally, and that ‘sed’ replaces with a special
2254: character.  This provides a way of encoding non-printable characters in
2255: patterns in a visible manner.  There is no restriction on the appearance
2256: of non-printing characters in a ‘sed’ script but when a script is being
2257: prepared in the shell or by text editing, it is usually easier to use
2258: one of the following escape sequences than the binary character it
2259: represents:
2260: 
2261:    The list of these escapes is:
2262: 
2263: ‘\a’
2264:      Produces or matches a BEL character, that is an “alert” (ASCII 7).
2265: 
2266: ‘\f’
2267:      Produces or matches a form feed (ASCII 12).
2268: 
2269: ‘\n’
2270:      Produces or matches a newline (ASCII 10).
2271: 
2272: ‘\r’
2273:      Produces or matches a carriage return (ASCII 13).
2274: 
2275: ‘\t’
2276:      Produces or matches a horizontal tab (ASCII 9).
2277: 
2278: ‘\v’
2279:      Produces or matches a so called “vertical tab” (ASCII 11).
2280: 
2281: ‘\cX’
2282:      Produces or matches ‘CONTROL-X’, where X is any character.  The
2283:      precise effect of ‘\cX’ is as follows: if X is a lower case letter,
2284:      it is converted to upper case.  Then bit 6 of the character (hex
2285:      40) is inverted.  Thus ‘\cz’ becomes hex 1A, but ‘\c{’ becomes hex
2286:      3B, while ‘\c;’ becomes hex 7B.
2287: 
2288: ‘\dXXX’
2289:      Produces or matches a character whose decimal ASCII value is XXX.
2290: 
2291: ‘\oXXX’
2292:      Produces or matches a character whose octal ASCII value is XXX.
2293: 
2294: ‘\xXX’
2295:      Produces or matches a character whose hexadecimal ASCII value is
2296:      XX.
2297: 
2298:    ‘\b’ (backspace) was omitted because of the conflict with the
2299: existing “word boundary” meaning.
2300: 
2301: 5.8.1 Escaping Precedence
2302: -------------------------
2303: 
2304: GNU ‘sed’ processes escape sequences _before_ passing the text onto the
2305: regular-expression matching of the ‘s///’ command and Address matching.
2306: Thus the following two commands are equivalent (‘0x5e’ is the
2307: hexadecimal ASCII value of the character ‘^’):
2308: 
2309:      $ echo 'a^c' | sed 's/^/b/'
2310:      ba^c
2311: 
2312:      $ echo 'a^c' | sed 's/\x5e/b/'
2313:      ba^c
2314: 
2315:    As are the following (‘0x5b’,‘0x5d’ are the hexadecimal ASCII values
2316: of ‘[’,‘]’, respectively):
2317: 
2318:      $ echo abc | sed 's/[a]/x/'
2319:      Xbc
2320:      $ echo abc | sed 's/\x5ba\x5d/x/'
2321:      Xbc
2322: 
2323:    However it is recommended to avoid such special characters due to
2324: unexpected edge-cases.  For example, the following are not equivalent:
2325: 
2326:      $ echo 'a^c' | sed 's/\^/b/'
2327:      abc
2328: 
2329:      $ echo 'a^c' | sed 's/\\\x5e/b/'
2330:      a^c
2331: 
2332:    ---------- Footnotes ----------
2333: 
2334:    (1) All the escapes introduced here are GNU extensions, with the
2335: exception of ‘\n’.  In basic regular expression mode, setting
[?2004hroot@4631ea5ed046:/app# python3 - <<'PY'
[?2004l[?2004h> p='/app/pysed/sed.py'
